            ].map((con) => (
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-red-800">
                <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-red-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
                  <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12" />
                </svg>
                {con}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <h2 id="comparison" class="scroll-mt-24">How It Compares</h2>
        <p>
          Because the M7 isn't a treadmill, "how it compares" means two
          different things here: how it stacks up against its own lineup
          sibling, and how it stacks up against an actual compact treadmill
          for readers still weighing the two categories against each other.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-gray-50 text-left">
              <th class="p-3 font-black text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200">Model</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-black text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200">Type</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-black text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200">Resistance</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-black text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200">Footprint (in use)</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-black text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-200">Price</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {COMPARISON.map((row, i) => (
              <tr class={`${i < COMPARISON.length - 1 ? "border-b border-gray-100" : ""} ${row.highlight ? "bg-[#0F62FE]/5" : ""}`}>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900">{row.name}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{row.type}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{row.resistance}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{row.footprint}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{row.price}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Against the <a href="/bowflex-max-trainer-m5">Bowflex Max Trainer M5</a>
          below it in Bowflex's own lineup, the M7 offers nearly double the
          resistance levels, a larger backlit console, and more complete
          JRNY app integration, for roughly $500-$600 more. If you're
          confident you'll use this machine seriously and progress in
          intensity over time, that gap is worth paying; if you're testing
          whether the Max Trainer format works for you at all, the M5 is
          the lower-risk way to find out.
        </p>
        <p>
          Against the <strong>XTERRA TRX3500</strong>, the compact folding
          treadmill we recommend in our
          <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews">folding treadmill guide</a>,
          the comparison really comes down to what kind of exercise you
          want. The TRX3500 lets you walk or run, has a considerably larger
          footprint even folded, and costs less than the M7. The M7 takes
          up dramatically less space, delivers a shorter, more intense
          workout format, and puts far less impact through your joints —
          but it cannot replace running if running is specifically what you
          want to do. Neither machine is objectively better; they're
          answering different questions about what you want your cardio
          time to look like.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-black text-amber-900 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v3.75m0 3.75h.007v.008H12v-.008zM21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path>
            </svg>
            Buying Tip
          </p>
          <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Bowflex frequently bundles a limited JRNY trial with new Max
            Trainer purchases. Try the full built-in console programs first
            before deciding whether the ongoing JRNY subscription is worth
            it for you — many testers found the standalone Max Interval
            program covered most of what they actually wanted.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">Who Should Skip This Machine</h2>
        <p>
          Skip the M7 if you specifically want to run. This is the single
          clearest disqualifier, and no amount of resistance levels or app
          integration changes it — the Max Trainer's motion simply isn't
          running, walking, or jogging, and it cannot be used to train for
          a race, maintain running mechanics, or replicate the specific
          feeling of running. If that's your goal, an actual treadmill like
          the <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews">XTERRA TRX3500</a>
          is the right purchase, not this machine.
        </p>
        <p>
          You should also skip it if long, steady-state cardio sessions are
          your preferred training style — the Max Trainer's design and
          built-in programs are built almost entirely around short,
          intense intervals, and it's a less natural fit for someone who
          wants to settle into a 45-minute steady effort. And if you're
          specifically budget-constrained, the M5 delivers most of the same
          core experience for several hundred dollars less, and is worth
          trying before committing to the M7's higher price.
        </p>

        <h2 id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          Judged honestly as what it actually is — a compact, low-impact,
          high-intensity interval training machine — the Bowflex Max
          Trainer M7 is a genuinely well-built piece of equipment. The
          resistance range gives real long-term progression, the footprint
          is a legitimate advantage for smaller spaces, and the workout
          intensity it delivers in a short amount of time is real, not
          marketing spin. What it is not, and what it will never be, is a
          treadmill, and we'd rather tell you that clearly than let you
          discover it after the return window closes.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you've read this far because you're weighing the M7 against a
          treadmill purchase, ask yourself one question honestly: do you
          actually want to run, or do you want efficient cardio and you've
          just been treadmill-shopping out of habit? If it's the former, buy
          a treadmill. If it's the latter, the M7 deserves serious
          consideration.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's also worth thinking about how this machine fits into a
          broader training routine rather than evaluating it purely as a
          standalone purchase. Several testers who already owned a
          treadmill found real value in adding an M7 alongside it — using
          the treadmill for longer, easier-paced sessions and the Max
          Trainer for short, hard interval days, splitting the joint
          impact across two different movement patterns rather than putting
          all of it through running. That's a more expensive path than
          picking one machine, but it's worth knowing the two aren't
          mutually exclusive if your budget and space allow for both.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 my-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Bowflex Max Trainer M7 a treadmill?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              No. The Max Trainer M7 is a compact hybrid cardio machine that
              combines elliptical glide and vertical stepper motion. It
              does not have a running belt and you cannot run or walk on it
              the way you would on a treadmill. We cover it here because
              it's one of the most-searched cardio machines by people also
              comparing treadmills, as a lower-impact alternative.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Max Trainer M7 worth it if I want a low-impact cardio machine?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Yes, for the right buyer. The M7's combined elliptical and
              stepper motion delivers a genuinely intense cardio workout in
              a short amount of time with far less joint impact than
              running, and its 14 resistance levels give you real room to
              progress. It's an excellent HIIT-style option specifically
              for people who want cardio intensity without the impact of
              running.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does the M7 compare to the M5?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The M7 has 14 resistance levels versus the M5's 8, a larger
              backlit console, and deeper JRNY app integration, for roughly
              $500-$600 more. The M5 uses a more basic LCD display and
              offers a shallower resistance range. If you're an
              intermediate or advanced user who wants long-term progression
              headroom, the M7 is worth the upgrade; casual or
              budget-conscious buyers are well served by the M5.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Max Trainer M7 require a subscription?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              No. The M7 functions fully as a standalone machine with its
              built-in Max Interval and Max Results programs. JRNY,
              Bowflex's coaching app, is optional and adds guided,
              auto-adjusting resistance workouts for a monthly fee, similar
              in concept to iFit on treadmills, but nothing about the
              machine's core function requires it.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy the M7 instead of a treadmill?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              Only if lower-impact cardio is actually what you're looking
              for. If your goal specifically includes running, training for
              a race, or replicating outdoor running mechanics, the M7
              cannot do that and a real treadmill like the XTERRA TRX3500 is
              the right purchase. If your goal is efficient, joint-friendly
              cardio and you don't need to run, the M7 is a legitimate and
              in some ways superior alternative.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much space does the Max Trainer M7 need?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
              The M7 has a 51 x 26-inch footprint when in use and folds
              down to roughly 34 x 26 inches for storage, meaningfully
              smaller than almost any treadmill, folding or fixed. That
              compact footprint is one of its biggest practical advantages
              for apartments and small home gyms.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

